"""Skeleton-owned lifecycle of one declared object or operation; internal.

A resource subclasses :class:`Lifecycle` through the collection's binding
(the rendered ``_binding.py``) and supplies the product steps as hooks. The
base owns the order and the result and failure contracts, so that no
resource implements them again:

``reconcile`` validates the declaration without a request (``identify``,
``declared``), connects, reads the object completely (``lookup``) and
projects it (``managed``), computes the minimal difference, runs the
resource's guards (``protect``) and predicts the result. Unless
``compatibility_always`` is set, an unchanged run returns without
discovering the product version; check mode returns after the eligibility
check. Otherwise ``recheck`` repeats a guard right before the writes.
After each write the object is read again by the same lookup and checked
(``verify``); a write never repeats.

A change is one ``write`` unless ``stages`` splits it into several, such as
creating an object disabled and enabling it, or disabling it before it is
deleted. Each stage names its step, the state its read-back must show
(``want`` and ``absent``) and its request; the stages run in order, and each
later one starts from the read-back of the one before (``record`` and
``current``). A lost reply is settled by its own stage's read-back, and a
failing stage ends the run with the outcome the earlier ones confirmed.

``compatibility_always`` decides when the version is checked: before the
first read when it is set, otherwise only before a write or a changed
prediction. A resource that learns the version from its first read, such as
a command runner that reports it with the data, checks right after that
read instead: it leaves the setting off and calls
``ensure_compatible(self.operation_id())`` at the end of ``lookup``. The
verdict is cached per operation, so the later check before a write repeats
nothing.

An operation that fits no resource sequence runs under ``execute(action)``.
It keeps the base's connection, probe policy and failure boundary and
supplies its own steps, settling each write through ``stage`` where a
read-back can prove it. Its input check goes into ``execute``'s
``validate``, which runs before the base connects, as ``identify`` and
``declared`` do for a resource, so invalid input is refused before a
locator or connection error can be. ``execute(action)`` is also the shape for a write
whose read-back is expensive, or impossible after a timeout because the
write may have spent the deadline: the action reports the outcome its own
evidence supports instead of reading back.

A reply lost after sending (category ``transport``, ``timeout`` or
``response``) proves nothing: the read-back alone decides. A read-back that
moved (``moved``) but misses the declaration fails with the outcome
changed; one that did not move fails with the outcome unknown. A definitive
refusal is reported as refused, under the step of the write it refused; the
read-back only tells whether something changed anyway. A write that the
client reports as never started (``started``) is unchanged. A field the
product never returns (``secret``) is always written, to an existing object
only with ``update_password: always``; it makes a successful write
``unverifiable`` and a lost one unknown. In a staged change, each stage
says whether its write sends such a field, so a lost reply to a stage that
sends none settles by its read-back alone.

Failures go through the binding's error schema with ``operation``,
``outcome`` (``unchanged``, ``unknown`` or ``changed``), the identity
options the schema accepts and the fields the failure names. A local
operation that raises ``OSError``, such as writing a file on the controller,
fails with category ``local`` and code ``operation_failed``, never with the
error's message, which may name private paths. Hooks keep their state on the
instance; a hook may refine ``operation`` within its step and may set
``confirmed`` when its own evidence proves an effect. ``changed`` tells
``protect`` and every later hook whether the declaration changes anything.

A subclass overrides hooks and settings and may add names of its own; it
never redefines the sequence, its helpers or the instance state, and a class
that does fails when it is created, at import:

- Hooks, methods to override: ``connect`` and ``compatibility`` (the
  binding), ``identify``, ``declared``, ``lookup``, ``managed``, ``same``,
  ``difference``, ``protect``, ``secret``, ``predict``, ``public``,
  ``diff``, ``recheck``, ``write``, ``stages``, ``started``, ``lost``,
  ``moved``, ``verify``, ``changed_fields``, ``finish``, ``operation_id``
  and ``identity_fields``; ``__init__`` for per-instance state, calling the
  base first.
- Settings, class attributes to set: ``schema``, ``result_key``,
  ``compatibility_id``, ``message``, ``state_option``,
  ``compatibility_always``, ``whole_diff``, ``recheck_code``,
  ``recheck_category``, ``identity_options`` and ``steps``.
- Reserved, never redefined: the sequence ``reconcile``, ``stage`` and
  ``execute``; its helpers ``step``, ``prepare``, ``ensure_compatible``,
  ``visible``, ``write_only`` and ``fail``; the instance state ``module``,
  ``params``, ``client``, ``operation``, ``attempted``, ``confirmed``,
  ``settling``, ``compatible``, ``absent``, ``key``, ``want``, ``record``,
  ``current``, ``delta`` and ``changed``, which hooks read and assign as
  their step requires.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from typing import Any, Callable

from ansible_collections.foundata.utils.plugins.module_utils import errors

LOSS = frozenset({"transport", "timeout", "response"})
"""Failure categories of a reply lost after the request may have been sent."""

HOOKS = frozenset(
    {
        "connect",
        "compatibility",
        "identify",
        "declared",
        "lookup",
        "managed",
        "same",
        "difference",
        "protect",
        "secret",
        "predict",
        "public",
        "diff",
        "recheck",
        "write",
        "stages",
        "started",
        "lost",
        "moved",
        "verify",
        "changed_fields",
        "finish",
        "operation_id",
        "identity_fields",
    }
)
"""Methods a subclass may override."""

SETTINGS = frozenset(
    {
        "schema",
        "result_key",
        "compatibility_id",
        "message",
        "state_option",
        "compatibility_always",
        "whole_diff",
        "recheck_code",
        "recheck_category",
        "identity_options",
        "steps",
    }
)
"""Class attributes a subclass may set."""

STATE = frozenset(
    {
        "module",
        "params",
        "client",
        "operation",
        "attempted",
        "confirmed",
        "settling",
        "compatible",
        "absent",
        "key",
        "want",
        "record",
        "current",
        "delta",
        "changed",
    }
)
"""Instance state the sequence owns; hooks assign it, no subclass redefines it."""

MISSING = object()

Stage = tuple[str, dict[str, Any], bool, Callable[[], None], bool]
"""One write of a change: step, wanted state, whether absent, request, and
whether that request sends a field ``secret`` names."""


class Failure(errors.ApiError):
    """A verdict the base reaches itself, such as a missing read-back."""

    def __init__(
        self, *, category: str, code: str, fields: list[str] | None = None
    ) -> None:
        super().__init__("Lifecycle verdict", category=category, code=code)
        self.fields = fields or []


class Lifecycle:
    """One module invocation; subclasses supply the product steps."""

    _sequence: tuple[str, ...] = ()
    """Reserved names a skeleton-owned extension adds; see __init_subclass__."""

    schema: Any = None
    """The collection's public error schema, set by the binding."""

    result_key = ""
    """Singular product key of the public object; empty for none."""

    compatibility_id = ""
    """Operation name for the compatibility check, without the state."""

    message = "Operation failed; see error_category and error_code"
    state_option = "state"
    """Option whose value ``absent`` requests deletion; empty for none."""

    compatibility_always = False
    """Check compatibility before product reads, including unchanged runs."""

    whole_diff = False
    """Publish the whole public object in a diff instead of changed fields."""

    recheck_code = ""
    """Refuse a moved record before writing when this error code is set."""

    recheck_category = "eligibility"
    identity_options: tuple[str, ...] = ()
    steps: tuple[tuple[str, str], ...] = ()
    """Base steps a resource reports under another public name."""

    def __init__(self, module: Any) -> None:
        self.module, self.params = module, module.params
        self.client: Any = None
        self.operation = "validate"
        self.attempted = self.confirmed = False
        self.settling = False
        self.changed = False
        self.compatible: set[str] = set()
        self.absent = bool(self.state_option) and (
            self.params.get(self.state_option) == "absent"
        )
        self.key: Any = None
        self.want: dict[str, Any] = {}
        self.record: Any = None
        self.current: Any = None
        self.delta: dict[str, Any] = {}

    def __init_subclass__(
        cls, *, sequence: tuple[str, ...] = (), **kwargs: Any
    ) -> None:
        """Refuse a subclass that redefines a reserved name.

        ``sequence`` is for the skeleton's own extensions of the sequence, such
        as act-once activation: they may define those reserved names, which
        stay reserved for their subclasses.
        """
        super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs)
        reserved = set(STATE) | (
            {name for name in vars(Lifecycle) if not name.startswith("__")}
            - HOOKS
            - SETTINGS
        )
        for base in cls.__mro__[1:]:
            reserved.update(vars(base).get("_sequence", ()))
        clash = sorted(set(vars(cls)) & (reserved - set(sequence)))
        if clash:
            raise TypeError(
                f"{cls.__qualname__} redefines names the lifecycle base reserves: "
                + ", ".join(clash)
                + "; rename them (see the _lifecycle module docstring)"
            )
        cls._sequence = tuple(sequence)

    # Binding: one implementation per collection.

    def connect(self) -> Any:
        """Build the client without a request or a command.

        ``prepare`` calls it before ``reconcile`` reads, and after a supplied
        action's ``validate`` but before the action, so building it must have
        no effect. A
        transport that must check its target before use does so where it
        builds its first command, not in a method that receives a command
        already built.
        """
        raise NotImplementedError

    def compatibility(self, operation: str) -> None:
        raise NotImplementedError

    # Product steps of a resource.

    def identify(self) -> Any:
        """Validate and canonicalize the identity options; no request."""
        return None

    def declared(self) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Validate the declared managed values; no request."""
        return {}

    def lookup(self) -> Any:
        """Read the object of ``self.key`` completely, or None when absent."""
        raise NotImplementedError

    def managed(self, record: Any) -> Any:
        """Project a stored object onto the comparable managed values."""
        return record

    def same(self, name: str, current: Any, desired: Any) -> bool:
        """Whether a stored managed value already satisfies the declared one."""
        return bool(current == desired)

    def difference(self) -> dict[str, Any]:
        if (
            self.record is not None
            and self.params.get("update_password") == "on_create"
        ):
            self.want = {k: v for k, v in self.want.items() if not self.secret(k)}
        current = self.current or {}
        return {
            key: value
            for key, value in self.want.items()
            if self.record is None
            or self.secret(key)
            or not self.same(key, current.get(key, MISSING), value)
        }

    def protect(self) -> None:
        """Refuse an ineligible change before the result is predicted."""

    def secret(self, name: str) -> bool:
        """Whether a managed field is written but never read back."""
        return False

    def predict(self) -> Any:
        return {**(self.current or {}), **self.visible()}

    def public(self, record: Any) -> Any:
        return self.managed(record)

    def diff(self) -> dict[str, Any] | None:
        if self.whole_diff:
            return {
                "before": self.current or {},
                "after": {} if self.absent else self.predict(),
            }
        if self.absent:
            return {"before": self.current, "after": {}}
        visible, current = self.visible(), self.current or {}
        before = {key: current[key] for key in visible if key in current}
        return {"before": before, "after": visible}

    def recheck(self) -> None:
        """Read again and refuse a moved record when recheck_code is configured."""
        if self.recheck_code and self.moved(self.lookup()):
            raise Failure(category=self.recheck_category, code=self.recheck_code)

    def write(self) -> None:
        raise NotImplementedError

    def stages(self) -> list[Stage]:
        """The writes of the declared change, in order; never empty.

        Each stage is ``(step, want, absent, request, secret)``: the step it
        runs under, the state its read-back must show, its request, and
        whether that request sends a field ``secret`` names. A lost reply to
        such a request leaves the secret unknown even when the read-back
        matches. The default is the single ``write`` under the step
        ``create``, ``update`` or ``delete`` with the declared ``want`` and
        ``absent``, sending every declared secret. A request reads ``record``
        and ``current`` when it runs, so a later stage sees the read-back of
        the stage before it.
        """
        step = (
            "delete" if self.absent else "create" if self.record is None else "update"
        )
        return [(step, self.want, self.absent, self.write, self.write_only())]

    def started(self) -> bool:
        """Whether a failed write may have reached the product."""
        return True

    def lost(self, failure: errors.ApiError) -> bool:
        """Whether read-back alone may settle this uncertain write response."""
        return failure.category in LOSS

    def moved(self, observed: Any) -> bool:
        """Whether read-back proves an effect relative to this stage's snapshot."""
        return (None if observed is None else self.managed(observed)) != self.current

    def changed_fields(self) -> list[str]:
        """Return public field names for an update, or an empty list otherwise."""
        return sorted(self.delta) if self.record is not None and not self.absent else []

    def finish(self, result: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:
        """Complete every successful result before execute passes it to exit_json.

        It runs after whatever ``execute`` ran: ``reconcile``, a supplied action
        and, in the appliance variant, ``act_once``. A class that offers several
        of them branches on its own state.
        """
        return result

    def verify(self, record: Any, desired: dict[str, Any], absent: bool) -> None:
        if absent:
            if record is not None:
                raise Failure(category="verification", code="still_present")
            return
        if record is None:
            raise Failure(category="verification", code="missing_after_write")
        observed = self.managed(record) or {}
        mismatch = sorted(
            key
            for key, value in desired.items()
            if not self.secret(key)
            and not self.same(key, observed.get(key, MISSING), value)
        )
        if mismatch:
            raise Failure(
                category="verification", code="not_persisted", fields=mismatch
            )

    # The sequence.

    def step(self, name: str) -> None:
        self.operation = dict(self.steps).get(name, name)

    def operation_id(self) -> str:
        if self.state_option:
            return f"{self.compatibility_id}:{self.params[self.state_option]}"
        return self.compatibility_id

    def ensure_compatible(self, operation: str) -> None:
        if operation not in self.compatible:
            self.compatibility(operation)
            self.compatible.add(operation)

    def prepare(self, operation: str) -> None:
        """Connect once and check compatibility when the binding's policy asks."""
        self.step("connect")
        if self.client is None:
            self.client = self.connect()
        if self.compatibility_always:
            self.step("compatibility")
            self.ensure_compatible(operation)

    def visible(self) -> dict[str, Any]:
        return {k: v for k, v in self.delta.items() if not self.secret(k)}

    def write_only(self) -> bool:
        return any(self.secret(key) for key in self.delta)

    def stage(self, request: Callable[[], None], secret: bool | None = None) -> Any:
        """Write once and settle against the current stage's declared state.

        Set record, current, want, delta and absent for this stage before calling.
        ``secret`` tells whether the request sends a field ``secret`` names;
        without it, the request counts as sending every declared secret.
        The returned read-back can seed the next stage. Confirmed progress survives
        a later refusal, uncertain response or request that never started.
        """
        self.attempted = True
        operation = self.operation
        sends = self.write_only() if secret is None else secret
        try:
            request()
        except errors.ApiError as failure:
            if not self.started():
                self.attempted = self.confirmed
                raise
            self.step("reconcile")
            self.settling = True
            observed = self.lookup()
            self.confirmed = self.confirmed or self.moved(observed)
            if not self.lost(failure):
                # The refusal belongs to the write, not to its read-back.
                self.attempted = self.confirmed or sends
                self.operation = operation
                raise
            self.verify(observed, self.want, self.absent)
            if sends:
                raise Failure(category="verification", code="create_unknown") from None
        else:
            self.step("verify")
            self.settling = True
            observed = self.lookup()
            self.confirmed = self.confirmed or self.moved(observed)
            self.verify(observed, self.want, self.absent)
        self.confirmed = True
        return observed

    def reconcile(self) -> dict[str, Any]:
        self.step("validate")
        self.key = self.identify()
        self.want = self.declared()
        self.prepare(self.operation_id())
        self.step("lookup")
        self.record = self.lookup()
        self.current = None if self.record is None else self.managed(self.record)
        self.delta = {} if self.absent else self.difference()
        self.changed = (
            self.record is not None
            if self.absent
            else self.record is None or bool(self.delta)
        )
        self.step("protect")
        self.protect()
        result: dict[str, Any] = {
            "changed": self.changed,
            "changed_fields": self.changed_fields(),
            "verified": "predicted"
            if self.module.check_mode and self.changed
            else "observed",
        }
        if self.result_key:
            result[self.result_key] = None if self.absent else self.predict()
        diff = self.diff() if self.module._diff and self.changed else None
        if diff is not None:
            result["diff"] = diff
        if not self.changed:
            return result
        self.step("compatibility")
        self.ensure_compatible(self.operation_id())
        if self.module.check_mode:
            return result
        self.step("recheck")
        self.recheck()
        plan = self.stages()
        if not plan:
            raise ValueError("stages() returned no write for a change")
        declared, absent = self.want, self.absent
        observed: Any = None
        for index, (step, want, gone, request, secret) in enumerate(plan):
            if index:
                self.record = observed
                self.current = None if observed is None else self.managed(observed)
            self.want, self.absent = want, gone
            self.step(step)
            observed = self.stage(request, secret)
        self.want, self.absent = declared, absent
        if self.result_key:
            result[self.result_key] = (
                None if observed is None else self.public(observed)
            )
        if self.write_only():
            result["verified"] = "unverifiable"
        return result

    # The catch boundary.

    def identity_fields(self) -> dict[str, Any]:
        return {
            name: self.params[name]
            for name in self.identity_options
            if self.params.get(name) is not None
        }

    def fail(self, failure: errors.ApiError) -> None:
        fields: dict[str, Any] = {
            "operation": self.operation,
            **self.identity_fields(),
        }
        named = getattr(failure, "fields", None)
        if named:
            # Objects an activation could not converge, otherwise fields.
            key = "not_effective" if failure.code == "not_effective" else "fields"
            fields[key] = named
        errors.fail_from(
            self.module,
            failure,
            schema=self.schema,
            msg=self.message,
            changed=self.attempted,
            outcome="changed"
            if self.confirmed
            else "unknown"
            if self.attempted
            else "unchanged",
            fields=fields,
        )

    def execute(
        self,
        action: Callable[[], dict[str, Any]] | None = None,
        *,
        compatibility_operation: str | None = None,
        validate: Callable[[], None] | None = None,
    ) -> None:
        """Run a resource or supplied operation under the failure boundary.

        A supplied action runs in this order. ``validate``, when given, checks
        the action's input without a request or a command and raises to
        refuse it, so invalid input fails before anything connects. Then
        ``prepare`` builds ``self.client`` and applies the compatibility
        policy for ``compatibility_operation`` (default ``operation_id()``),
        so the action starts connected and never connects itself. A resource
        whose transport is not the binding's client, such as a command
        runner, overrides ``connect`` to return it. A refusal by ``validate``
        reports the operation the instance had, ``validate`` or what
        ``__init__`` set, and that operation is restored after preparing.
        """
        if validate is not None and action is None:
            raise ValueError("validate checks a supplied action's input")
        try:
            if action is not None:
                operation = self.operation
                if validate is not None:
                    validate()
                self.prepare(compatibility_operation or self.operation_id())
                self.operation = operation
            sequence = action if action is not None else self.reconcile
            result = self.finish(sequence())
        except errors.ApiError as failure:
            self.fail(failure)
        except OSError:
            self.fail(Failure(category="local", code="operation_failed"))
        else:
            self.module.exit_json(**result)
